  "statement": "Write down the expansion of $(1+x)^{-1/2}$ up to the term in $x^{3}$, and use it to approximate $\\\\tfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1.02}}$ and $\\\\tfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{0.98}}$.",
  "solution": "$(1+x)^{-1/2}=1-\\\\tfrac12x+\\\\tfrac38x^{2}-\\\\tfrac{5}{16}x^{3}+\\\\cdots$. For $\\\\tfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1.02}}=(1+0.02)^{-1/2}\\\\approx 1-0.01+\\\\tfrac38(0.0004)-\\\\tfrac{5}{16}(0.000008)=0.99005-0.0000025\\\\approx0.99005$. For $\\\\tfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{0.98}}=(1-0.02)^{-1/2}\\\\approx 1+0.01+\\\\tfrac38(0.0004)+\\\\tfrac{5}{16}(0.000008)=1.010025+0.0000025\\\\approx1.01003$.",
  "solutionStatus": "full",
  "hints": [
    "Use $(1+x)^{n}$ with $n=-\\\\tfrac12$.",
    "Substitute $x=\\\\pm0.02$."
  ],
